r219 amendment (issue #274, Windows) -- the pool's fill must never require a tier the platform
does not have, and a FAILED fill must not be resident:

  * the pool's DISK TIER (MOE_HOST_POOL_DIO=1) is best-effort.  `pool_open_file` is a compile-time
    stub on Windows, so requesting O_DIRECT there failed EVERY pool fill, which failed every POOL
    (table_pool_locked() -> null, i.e. "the pool never appeared"), and the bounce fallback asked for
    the same missing tier and failed too ("no pinned host source ... (pool and bounce both
    unavailable)").  The mapping is always a valid source, so `pool_fill_slot_locked`,
    `expert_host_src` and `pool_mirror_bulk_fill_locked` fall back to it, and `pool_dio_effective()`
    refuses a disk tier the platform lacks with one WARN.  The pool, its pin and the in-place cold
    read are unchanged.
  * `access_locked()` recorded the slot as RESIDENT before the copy, so a fill that could not be
    serviced served stale arena bytes (`async fill failed ...: no error` -- cudaGetLastError() is
    empty for a host-side failure): 20 t/s prefill and a ONE-token answer on the reporter's box.  The
    admission now rolls back, the table is marked `fill_failed` and stands down wholesale (the same
    policy as `alloc_failed`), so the scheduler serves the op from the host master, and the census
    reports `FAILED fills: N`.
  * the tier census (`report_host_expert_placement`) is WARN, not INFO -- at the default verbosity
    INFO does not exist, which is why the reporter saw every failure and none of the decisions --
    and it carries the pool-source count.
  * diagnostics: MOE_HOST_POOL_DIO_FAULT (a platform with no pool disk tier) and
    MOE_HOST_POOL_FILL_FAULT (no source at all), the two arms `scripts/gate-host-pool.sh` uses to
    reach the Windows shape and the rollback from Linux.

Gates: gate-host-pool.sh 19/19 (four arms byte-identical; pre-fix 7281 `async fill failed` lines and
garbled text); see WORKLOG.md 261105.005.

  * r220, cross-backend copy ordering (from @slavap, PR #268): the general D2D split-input copy used
    the backend's SHARED per-device `copy_event` -- issue on the source stream, re-record that event,
    wait the destination stream on it.  A queued HIP stream wait resolves against the LATEST record at
    execution, so a copy still in flight re-records the event and re-binds the consumers already
    queued on it; independent copies then serialize against each other.  `cpy_tensor_async`'s body is
    now `cpy_tensor_async_impl(..., ev_nowait)` and the new optional iface member
    `cpy_tensor_async_nowait` takes a CALLER-owned event, recorded on the source stream right behind
    the copy and waited on by the consumer, so no wait is ever re-bound.  The scheduler keeps a ring
    of those events per source backend (a slot is re-recorded only after its previous record
    completed) and restarts the rings at every full synchronize.  `GGML_SCHED_COPY_EVENT=shared`
    restores the old path (kill switch).  Bit-exact and throughput-neutral on this box (same-seed
    greedy text identical on `-sm tensor` / `-sm layer` / `-sm layer -ncmoe`; pp8192 3-GPU `-sm layer`
    6311 vs 6317 t/s); the win is on a box where two cross-backend copies overlap.
